92186045417] [17592186064093 #edb/ref 1074 "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064094 #edb/ref 1069 "e-474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064094 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064094 #edb/ref 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064095 #edb/ref 1069 "e-475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064095 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b34db891-0676-505c-9ba3-57d86f1d9b94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064095 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the identity $\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref